---
import BaseLayout from '~/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Section from '~/components/Section.astro';
import Button from '~/components/Button.astro';
import ImpactStats from '~/components/ImpactStats.astro';

/**
 * HOME PAGE.
 *
 * The hero is the decided tagline — "Food is a human right." — set alone, with
 * no trailing qualifier. It is a belief statement, so the lead paragraph
 * underneath it does the work of saying who we are and what we actually do.
 *
 * Positioning this page has to hold, in this order:
 *   1. Share Meals is a nonprofit dedicated to food security. Not "an app".
 *      Technology is one of the tools; it is never the headline.
 *   2. Colleges are the roots and still the main service population, but they
 *      are credibility, not scope — campus appears in body copy and never in
 *      the tagline or an H1, so the brand is not siloed to it.
 *   3. Non-tech direct service is a side note, mentioned once, not a section.
 *
 * Copy follows the dignity-first language rule: food that is shared is food,
 * and the mission is framed as ensuring food security rather than against
 * insecurity.
 *
 * The closing band of figures comes from the backend at runtime and disappears
 * entirely if the numbers cannot be fetched — see ImpactStats.astro. Nothing
 * on this page should be written to depend on it being there.
 *
 * The calls to action point at /app and /pantry-point, and they sit in the
 * second band rather than the hero on purpose. Positioning rule 1 above is the
 * reason: a product button directly under the tagline makes the product the
 * headline, which is the one thing this page is not supposed to do. In the
 * second band they arrive after the sentence that introduces the work, as the
 * answer to it.
 *
 * There were two buttons only once there were two pages worth pointing at. The
 * standing rule is that a button going nowhere is worse than no button, so
 * anything else this page might advertise stays off it until it is real. See
 * the note on the buttons themselves for what their arrival did to the
 * sentence above them.
 */
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Share Meals — food is a human right"
  description="Share Meals is a nonprofit dedicated to food security. Some of that work is software. Some of it is showing up with food."
  bareTitle
>
  <Section size="hero">
    <h1 class="text-display-2xl font-extrabold text-balance m-0">
      Food is a<br />
      <span class="text-[var(--color-brand-700)]">human right.</span>
    </h1>

    <p class="text-lead mt-8 max-w-2xl text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">
      Share Meals is a nonprofit dedicated to food security. Some of that work
      is software. Some of it is showing up with food.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section tone="inverse">
    <h2 class="text-display font-extrabold text-balance m-0">
      Believing it is the easy part.
    </h2>
    <p class="text-lead mt-6 max-w-2xl">
      A right that exists on paper is not a meal in anyone's hands. Closing that
      distance is the whole of our work: software that shows people the food
      already around them, and programs that put food where software alone
      won't reach.
    </p>
    <p class="text-lead mt-6 max-w-2xl">
      We began with college students, and campuses are still where most of our
      work happens. They are not where it stops. A right that only holds on
      campus was never a right.
    </p>

    {/* Link text has to stand on its own — read out of context in a list of
        links, "learn more" says nothing about where it goes.

        TWO BUTTONS NOW, WHERE THE NOTE ABOVE SAYS ONE.

        The rule that produced the single button was "a button that goes
        nowhere is worse than no button", and the reason there was one was that
        /app was the only page to point at. /pantry-point is a real page now,
        so the condition that made this a single-button band has expired rather
        than been overruled. The button stays out of the hero for the original
        reason, which has not changed: a product button directly under the
        tagline makes the product the headline, and positioning rule 1 says it
        never is.

        Equal weight is deliberate. Ranking them here would be the home page
        making a claim about which programme matters more, and it has no
        business doing that — each page argues its own case, including
        /pantry-point's, which is explicit that its mechanism is history.

        TODO(content): the sentence above these buttons splits our work in two —
        "software that shows people the food already around them, and programs
        that put food where software alone won't reach". Pantry Point is
        genuinely neither half: it does not surface food that already exists,
        and it is not the non-tech direct service the second half was written
        for. The buttons are honest and the destination pages explain
        themselves, so this is not blocking, but that sentence was written when
        there was one product and it now has to carry two. Worth a rewrite by
        whoever owns the positioning. */}
    <ul class="mt-10 m-0 flex flex-wrap gap-4 p-0 list-none">
      <li><Button href="/app" variant="inverse">See the Share Meals app</Button></li>
      <li><Button href="/pantry-point" variant="inverse">See Pantry Point</Button></li>
    </ul>
  </Section>

  <ImpactStats />
</BaseLayout>
